#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185006} :activity/id #uuid "af91633b-288c-52bd-a641-7172621608f6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049327 :topic/id #uuid "ec1d59b5-66a8-5507-9862-10567e3ce6ab" :topic/math-academy-id 3834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184078 :question/id #uuid "7dbb258d-fe71-5e7b-9501-c88ffdb6fe86" :question/math-academy-id "e-629"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2216cdfc-7f2a-5c53-b10b-2d66f35fbf32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542229}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Tangent Lines to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a0ead2f8-7726-5a22-ad4d-582c7b4b8968" :step/math-academy-id 642669 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184215 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184120 :question/id #uuid "59156f24-72a1-5642-ac66-f84002c87d0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9955"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e95ede7-306b-55e1-9245-7493f0afb514" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542206}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Normal Lines to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "e6c2d34a-6683-50c4-a82e-b71e985d4ed8" :step/math-academy-id 642672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185342 :tutorial/content "![](images/a0/a0b4ad67ea80f6d6eec3a15b3df18d551d84263c7424405588bd15162ef4e7c2.png)\n\nThe polar curve shown above is defined as\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 4 - 3\\cos \\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ at the point $P.$ The tangent to the curve at $P$ is shown in the diagram. \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at $P,$ we follow four steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$\n\nUsing techniques that we've previously discussed, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3}{0-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( 0, 4 \\right).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "febc7b49-9662-5e78-a7a5-cbe9b1f596ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a1e51e4b-71cf-5749-aae1-c001b3a5a59b" :step/math-academy-id 642668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184944}}] :activity/title "Tangent and Normal Lines to Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184419}]}
